## Problem

`erg ready` currently promotes the "claimed" branch-observation signal to a
blocker: ready.go:264 does `if claimed { blocked = true }`, which exiles claimed
tickets out of the ready list and into a separate "Claimed tickets" section.

This conflates two distinct questions:

  - **ready** — prerequisites satisfied, the ticket is actionable.
  - **claimed** — someone may already be working on it (informational signal).

A claimed-but-otherwise-ready ticket genuinely is ready. Filtering it out is
workflow opinion, not a spec requirement. spec-erg-v1.md:143 says ready "looks
at existing branches names to see if work is already happening" — observation,
not filtering. pep-erg-v1.md §6 confirms branch names are a *signal*, with
coordination explicitly out of scope.

## Plan

  - ready.go: remove the `blocked = true` promotion when claimed. Keep the
    branch lookup (still lazy per closed 0079). Annotate claimed entries with
    a `[claimed]` marker in the human output; drop the separate Claimed
    section.
  - JSON schema unchanged (id, title, file, ready, claimed, tags, blocked_by).
    Semantics shift for claimed entries: ready=true (was false) and claimed=true.
    Consumers detecting claimed via ready=false must switch to claimed=true —
    which is what the field is for.
  - tests/test_ready.sh: flip the "claimed → ready=false" assertion to
    "claimed → ready=true and claimed=true"; replace the "Claimed section"
    assertion with a "[claimed] marker" check.
  - helpReady: rewrite the claimed paragraph to describe marker semantics.
  - STATE.md:29: note that the claimed-as-blocker divergence is resolved.

## Acceptance

- [ ] Claimed ticket appears in the ready list with a `[claimed]` marker.
- [ ] No separate "Claimed tickets" section in human output.
- [ ] JSON: claimed entries have ready=true and claimed=true.
- [ ] loadBranchNames remains lazy (only invoked when there is a ready
      candidate to mark).
- [ ] test_ready.sh updated and passing.
- [ ] helpReady and manual reflect marker semantics.
